{"id":"housing-lottery-odds","title":"Housing lotteries, in aggregate","question":"How many applications chase each below-market-rate home, and how do the odds differ between preference categories?","finding":"74 listings on the City’s housing portal offered 689 homes and drew 128,010 applications; the middle listing had 260 applications per home and the most contested 2,925. Holders of a Certificate of Preference filed 0.44 applications for each home open to them, and in 50 of 74 listings there were at least as many such homes as certificate applicants; live-or-work applicants filed 101 per home. In every one of the 74 listings applicants with no preference could not reach a home by arithmetic, because applicants holding a preference alone outnumbered the homes.","how":"One row per listing that has preference counts in the portal’s data. Applications are the live-or-work count plus the general pool, which together match the number of tickets printed in the results files to within 1%. Each preference’s figure is its applications divided by the homes open to it. \"Left for live-or-work applicants\" takes the homes and subtracts, in the order the portal lists them, the smaller of each earlier preference’s applications and its reserved homes; \"left for no preference\" subtracts the live-or-work applications as well. Listings named only by a street address with fewer than five homes are not identified.","limits":"These are counts of applications, not people housed: results files list ticket numbers, and the portal data shows only the top ticket in each preference, so who was actually placed is not public. The arithmetic assumes every preference applicant qualifies and accepts, which overstates how many homes preferences take: the Budget and Legislative Analyst’s 2022 report (in the reviews table) found many applications were being reviewed for each home filled. One household can hold several preferences and is counted in each. The portal exposes only listings still live, mostly from 2025 and 2026. Preferences are the program’s published rules. The rule: “there is no separate drawing for each pool, applicants receive only one lottery rank number which determines their position on each preference list.”","tier":"REDFLAG","columns":[{"k":"listing","t":"Listing","type":"text"},{"k":"lotteryDate","t":"Lottery date","type":"date"},{"k":"homes","t":"Homes","type":"int"},{"k":"applications","t":"Applications","type":"int"},{"k":"perHome","t":"Applications per home","type":"times"},{"k":"copApps","t":"Certificate of Preference applications","type":"int"},{"k":"copHomes","t":"Homes open to them","type":"int"},{"k":"dthpApps","t":"Displaced-tenant applications","type":"int"},{"k":"dthpHomes","t":"Homes reserved","type":"int"},{"k":"nrhpApps","t":"Neighborhood-resident applications","type":"int"},{"k":"nrhpHomes","t":"Homes reserved","type":"int"},{"k":"liveWorkApps","t":"Live-or-work applications","type":"int"},{"k":"leftForLiveWork","t":"Homes left for them","type":"int"},{"k":"liveWorkPerHome","t":"Live-or-work applications per home left","type":"times"},{"k":"generalApps","t":"No-preference applications","type":"int"},{"k":"leftForNoPreference","t":"Homes left for them","type":"int"}],"rows":[{"listing":"Modera Rincon Hill","lotteryDate":"2026-09-02","status":"Lottery Complete","homes":1,"applications":2925,"perHome":2925.0,"copApps":11,"copHomes":1,"dthpApps":0,"dthpHomes":0,"nrhpApps":null,"nrhpHomes":null,"liveWorkApps":2491,"generalApps":434,"leftForLiveWork":0,"liveWorkPerHome":null,"leftForNoPreference":0},{"listing":"Brick + Iron Lofts at Dogpatch","lotteryDate":"2026-06-04","status":"Lottery Complete","homes":1,"applications":2499,"perHome":2499.0,"copApps":4,"copHomes":1,"dthpApps":13,"dthpHomes":1,"nrhpApps":null,"nrhpHomes":null,"liveWorkApps":2114,"generalApps":385,"leftForLiveWork":0,"liveWorkPerHome":null,"leftForNoPreference":0},{"listing":"O&M Apartments","lotteryDate":"2026-06-02","status":"Lottery Complete","homes":1,"a